# Gettysburg National Military Park, Pennsylvania (National Park Service Historical Handbook Series No. 9; Frederick Tilberg; Washington, 1954)
# A popular handbook by the park historian (a secondary summary without citations): only its plain statements about the field in July 1863, the hospitals and the later enclosure of the cemetery are extracted.
# The 1962 edition (NPS-HB9-1962) repeats the narrative and is not extracted separately here.
@ NPS-HB9-1954

33/29 | fences | Pickett's troops halted at the Emmitsburg Road to remove fence rails | FEN-emmitsburg-road | 1863-07-03 | stated | The handbook says that halted momentarily at the Emmitsburg Road to remove fence rails, Pickett's troops, with Pettigrew on the left, renewed the advance | #s47
34/30 | fields | Tall grass a hundred yards from the stone wall, where Union skirmishers lay | - | 1863-07-03 | stated | The handbook says that a hundred yards from the stone wall, in the tall grass, the Confederates met Union skirmishers who fired and withdrew | #s5
34/30 | fences | Armistead reached the low stone fence at the Angle | P-01,E-17,FEN-cemetery-ridge-walls | 1863-07-03 | stated | The handbook says Armistead reached the low stone fence | #s10
36/33 | hospitals | The General Hospital east of Gettysburg took the wounded of both armies from the field hospitals a few weeks after the battle | - | 1863-07 to 1863-08 (a few weeks after the battle) | stated | The handbook says that a few weeks after the battle the Union and Confederate wounded were removed to the General Hospital one mile east of Gettysburg from field hospitals in the rear of the battle lines | #s3-4
49/46 | hospitals | Old Dorm at Gettysburg College was used as a hospital during the battle | bld-schmucker-hall | 1863-07 | stated | The handbook says the large white building on the near side of Gettysburg is Old Dorm at Gettysburg College, used as a hospital during the battle | #s9
50/47 | fields | The Wheatfield, the open ground surrounded by woods, and the Peach Orchard near the white buildings on the ridge (the visitor's view of 1954) | bld-trostle-farm,bld-sherfy-farm | ~1954 (the handbook's description of the ground as the visitor saw it; not dated to 1863) | stated | The handbook's stop text names the Wheatfield as the open ground surrounded by woods and places the Peach Orchard near the white buildings on the ridge, in the sentence on the Confederate gains | #s20
51/48 | damage | The Leister house and barn were badly damaged by the artillery fire before Pickett's Charge | bld-leister-house | 1863-07-03 | stated | The handbook says the Leister house and barn were badly damaged by the artillery fire that preceded Pickett's Charge | #s22
39/36 | later | The Soldiers' National Cemetery enclosed with a stone wall and an iron fence on the Baltimore Street front, iron gateways and a keeper's lodge within a year | - | !1864 (within a year of the cemetery's dedication in 1863) | stated | The handbook says that within a year appropriations from the states made possible the enclosure of the cemetery with a massive stone wall and an iron fence on the Baltimore Street front, iron gateways, headstones and a keeper's lodge | #s6
